Host:    Apple Silicon (aarch64), darwin 25.5.0
Command: cargo bench --bench kernel_tiers   (no -C target-cpu=native)
Harness: fast-ssim2/benches/kernel_tiers.rs
Method:  archmage NeonToken::dangerously_disable_token_process_wide toggles the
         tier between arms in one process, so both arms run on identical data
         with identical cache state.

WHY THIS EXISTS
  fast-ssim2 had only end-to-end measurement (1.7-1.8x on aarch64). An
  end-to-end number cannot reveal a single kernel that is SLOWER than its own
  scalar fallback -- the faster kernels average it away. That exact failure
  mode was found and fixed in garb, zensim, zentone, zenpng and zenresize
  during the same 2026-07-28 aarch64 sweep, so fast-ssim2 was checked the same
  way rather than assumed healthy.

RESULT: no losers. Every compute-bound kernel wins 1.76x-2.68x.

  image_multiply's 0.99x is NOT a gap. At 1920x1080 it touches
  3 planes x 3 arrays x 4 B x 2.07 Mpx = 74.6 MB in 577 us = ~129 GB/s,
  at/above this host's measured memory bandwidth (zenbench calibration
  reported 117.8 GiB/s). Both arms are pinned at the bandwidth ceiling, so
  there is no arithmetic left for SIMD to remove. The crate's own
  hand-written scalar reference lands at the same 569-576 us, confirming it.

FUSION LEVER CONSIDERED AND REJECTED (measured, not assumed)
  lib.rs:621-628 (and strip.rs:550/1067) call image_multiply three times on
  the same two inputs (img1*img1, img2*img2, img1*img2), each followed by a
  blur. Fusing the three into one pass would cut this stage's memory traffic
  from ~7 to ~5 plane-touches (~1.4x). But the stage is ~1.7 ms of a >33 ms
  pipeline (5 blurs at 6.2 ms each dominate), so the ceiling is ~1.5% -- and
  it costs three live output buffers instead of one reused `mul` (+50 MB at
  1080p). Bad trade; not shipped.
